# Relationship Fields in Salesforce: Types of Relationship in Salesforce

## Summary

Relationship fields connect Salesforce objects, allowing users to easily access related records. Relationships are created by adding a **relationship field** to the **child object**.

Schema Builder provides a visual representation of these relationships, making it easier to understand the data model.

---

## Key Concepts

### Why Use Relationships?

- Associate one object with another.
- Allow users to navigate between related records.
- Organize business data logically.
- Improve the overall data model.

---

### Relationship Categories

Salesforce supports two main relationship categories:

- **One-to-Many (1:N)**
- **Many-to-Many (M:N)**

---

### One-to-Many Relationship Types

#### Lookup Relationship

- Loosely coupled relationship.
- Parent and child records exist independently.
- Child record references one parent.
- One parent can have many child records.

#### Master-Detail Relationship

- Tightly coupled relationship.
- Covered in the next lesson.

---

### Other Relationship Types

- External Lookup
- Indirect Lookup
- Hierarchical

These relationship types exist but are less common for the Platform Developer I exam.

---

### Parent and Child Objects

Every one-to-many relationship contains:

- **Parent** (the "one")
- **Child** (the "many")

**Important**

The relationship field is **always created on the child object.**

Example:

```
Position
    │
    ├── Job Application
    ├── Job Application
    └── Job Application
```

The **Job Application** object stores the relationship field that points to **Position**.

---

## Certification Tips

- Relationship fields are created on the **child object**.
- One parent can have multiple child records.
- Lookup relationships are **loosely coupled**.
- Schema Builder is useful for visualizing relationships.
- Many-to-many relationships are implemented differently (covered later).

